mknod - make a device node

SYNOPSIS
    mknod NAME TYPE MAJOR MINOR

DESCRIPTION
    A device node is a file whose contents are a number. The number says
    which driver answers for it - a major, which names the driver, and a
    minor, which says which of the things that driver handles.

        mknod /tmp/nowhere c 1 3
        echo hello > /tmp/nowhere

    That second file behaves exactly as /dev/null does, because it is
    /dev/null: the identity is the pair 1,3 on the inode, not the path.
    The C library used to decide by comparing the path against a list of
    five strings, which meant a second name for a device did nothing and
    this command could not have existed.

    TYPE is c for a character device - one that moves bytes, like a
    terminal - or b for a block device, one that moves fixed-size blocks
    and can be seeked, like a disk.

    Nothing checks that a driver with that major number exists. A node is
    made before its driver is loaded as often as after, and a system that
    refused to name a device it could not currently reach could not
    describe its own disks while repairing them.

    Root only. The four bytes are harmless; the claim they make about
    which driver answers is not.

    The numbers are the ones Linux uses, so a node means the same thing on
    either system: 1,3 is null, 1,5 is zero, 1,7 is full, 5,0 is the
    controlling terminal and 5,1 is the console.

    ls shows one with c or b in the first column of a long listing, and
    prints the two numbers where a file would have its size.

SEE ALSO
    ls, mkfifo, stat
